artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider lattice QCD at finite baryon chemical potential $\mu$, where the Euclidean path integral

$$Z(\mu) = \int \mathcal{D}U \, \det M(U,\mu)\, e^{-S_g(U)}$$

has a complex fermion determinant $\det M(U,\mu) \in \mathbb{C}$ for $\mu \neq 0$, so that the integrand cannot be interpreted as a probability weight (the "sign problem"). A variety of numerical approaches—reweighting from $\mu=0$, Taylor expansion in $\mu/T$, analytic continuation from imaginary chemical potential, complex Langevin dynamics, Lefschetz-thimble integration, density-of-states methods, and others—are formally correct: under their stated mathematical assumptions they would converge to the exact expectation values $\langle \mathcal{O}\rangle_\mu = Z(\mu)^{-1}\int \mathcal{D}U\, \mathcal{O}\, \det M\, e^{-S_g}$. However, in finite-statistics simulations these methods can silently produce systematically biased or unphysical results due to phenomena such as:
(i) overlap problems, where the simulated ensemble has exponentially small overlap with the target ensemble at $\mu \neq 0$;
(ii) uncontrolled fluctuations of the complex phase $e^{i\theta(U,\mu)}$ of the determinant, producing severe sign cancellations;
(iii) for stochastic complexification schemes (e.g.\ complex Langevin), insufficient decay of the probability distribution in the non-compact directions of the complexified configuration space, invalidating the formal proof of correctness;
(iv) for thimble decompositions, missed contributing thimbles or unresolved Stokes phenomena.

The problem is the following. Devise a set of robust, practically computable diagnostic criteria $\{D_i(\mathcal{E})\}$, evaluable from the simulated ensemble $\mathcal{E}$ alone (without knowledge of the exact answer), such that:

(a) if all $D_i$ lie within explicitly specified tolerances, then the computed expectation values $\langle \mathcal{O}\rangle_\mu$ are guaranteed (with quantifiable confidence) to be free of the systematic artifacts (i)–(iv) and to lie within stated error bars of the true value;
(b) if any $D_i$ falls outside its tolerance, the diagnostic identifies which failure mode is responsible;
(c) the criteria apply uniformly across the formally-correct methods listed above and are sharp enough to distinguish trustworthy from untrustworthy results in regimes (such as $\mu/T \gtrsim 1$ in the cold/dense regime of QCD) where current heuristics are known to be inadequate.

Construct such diagnostics, or prove that no diagnostic with properties (a)–(c) exists within a specified computational class.

